WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.953 --> 00:00:09.203
(patriotic band music)

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.463
- Hello, Yale.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.700
- Wonderful.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:39.700
So I want to introduce
that co-moderating this Q&amp;A

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.480
will be Professor Albert Laguna,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.160
an associate professor in
ethnicity, race and migration,

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.250
and Professor Quan Tran.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:50.870
It will start with a few questions,

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.140
and then there will also
be the opportunity for you,

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.740
the audience, to pose
questions to Kristina.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.173
All right, I'll turn it
over to you two, Quan.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:01.330
- All right, thank you Diana.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.650
Thank you, Kristina, for
that tremendous performance.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.340
It took me on a rollercoaster of emotions,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.910
and speaks to the power of culture,

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.010
and the power of our vote.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:15.180
Very much inspired by this.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.020
So why don't we start with just

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.140
a broad question?

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.530
If you could let us know how

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:26.436
your path to comedy and
performance happened,

00:01:26.436 --> 00:01:29.036
that would be a great opening
for this conversation.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.970
- Huh, well (laughs)

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:37.460
I actually joke that a lot
of how I ended up doing this

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:40.460
is because I could never find a...

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:43.160
I wasn't actually allowed
to see a therapist

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.190
when I was a kid.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.990
It was very shameful, and so

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.920
being seen publicly was
like its own therapy.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.180
And I actually imagined out of college

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.860
that I was gonna be a very
serious performance artist

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:59.220
doing dark and twisted things in galleries

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.143
and moving slowly and it would,

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:04.410
and long durational work.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.197
And people would respond
to that work with,

00:02:06.197 --> 00:02:07.510
"You're so funny."

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.410
And I was like, "That was my soul.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:10.640
What was that?"

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.270
So I just began to be introduced

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.700
more and more in spaces as a comedian.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.650
It was a very scary term to embrace

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:21.180
because I feel like there are
very commercial expectations

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.940
of saying you're a comedian.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.280
There's very...

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.020
It has to look like this, and
you have to make me laugh,

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.410
and you don't have to be on all the time.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.243
But I think

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.583
that's not true.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.210
This is just what my comedy looks like,

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:35.043
and it's what it is.

00:02:35.043 --> 00:02:37.340
And if it doesn't fit your vision of it,

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.700
go away because I'm at this
point too old to try to

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.380
wrestle with making you happy.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.150
It's only in the last
10 years I've been like,

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.806
okay, I'm gonna tell people
I'm a comedian. (laughs)

00:02:49.806 --> 00:02:51.300
And it means sometimes
having a conversation

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.133
when people invite me.

00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:53.610
Can you do five minutes on a mic?

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.363
And I'm like, "Not really because I have

00:02:55.363 --> 00:02:57.307
this whole thing I do."

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:00.660
But yeah, that's been the journey.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.113
But a lot of it has came
out of coping, honestly.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.340
- [Albert] So Kristina, I teach the class

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.900
on race and comedy, and it's a tough class

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.700
because I feel like students come excited.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:17.620
We're gonna talk about comedy.

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.560
Then it's more and more
depressing as the weeks go by

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:21.393
as they'll get the history of

00:03:21.393 --> 00:03:23.193
racial representation in comedy.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.010
I'd love for you to talk about

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:29.040
the strengths and limitations of comedy

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.750
for thinking about,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.103
for thinking about race.

00:03:33.571 --> 00:03:37.790
- (sighs) Okay, so this is something

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.530
I feel like I have dealt
with in a lot of my shows,

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:43.790
and why I tend to gravitate
towards more theater spaces is

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.760
I think there's a patience
in a theater or gallery space

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.680
that there is not in a
commercial comedy space,

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.660
and I feel like in a
commercial comedy space

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:57.010
I have to play to a very
lowest common denominator,

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:00.630
and give my mother an
accent she doesn't have,

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.770
or you know your Chinese when.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:07.440
And I feel like in theater, the reason...

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.280
Most of my shows, I feel
like the first 20 minutes

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.890
is me teaching an audience
how to watch the show and me.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.990
And you don't really
get afforded that time.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:18.960
Okay, let me very quickly sum up

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:20.040
why it is I'm speaking English

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.410
and why it is this is my worldview

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:22.700
and why it is I'm a performance artist

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:24.210
and why it...

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.060
This one I think I moved very quickly

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:28.790
and I'm hoping the audience
gets pulled along for the ride

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:31.561
and are not like, "Why
isn't she a doctor?"

00:04:31.561 --> 00:04:33.430
They're stuck on narratives that they've

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:34.483
watched me play out.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:36.413
And I don't have time for that.

00:04:36.413 --> 00:04:37.690
I've got to tell you about
neighborhood council.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.130
I have no time for that. (laughs)

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:41.310
I'm just hoping you just
come along for the ride.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.530
But I do feel like
there's lot more prefacing

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.240
that people of color need to do

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.320
to give a little rubric
for how they're read

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.233
that white comedians
don't have to deal with.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.250
Some people just have to
explain what a Muslim is,

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:59.110
or just have to explain (laughs)

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:02.730
just basic things that aren't taught

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.223
in the world of culture.

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:10.340
- Great. Thank you, Kristina.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:14.040
That helped me raise the next
question in the sense that

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.840
you have characterized your
work as guerrilla performance

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:19.920
and a form of cultural jamming.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:21.950
Could you elaborate a little
bit on these concepts,

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.380
and why you're drawn to them?

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.440
- [Kristina] Yeah, so

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:29.150
and this ties back into the
TV pilot that I described,

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:31.640
which was gonna be this
whole guerrilla thing.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:35.440
But I think very much I'm
informed by the context that

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:36.380
I grew up in San Francisco.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:37.570
I live in Los Angeles.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:40.110
I had the privilege of
having been surrounded by

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.080
a lot of Asian-American
centered communities.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.720
So for me to do things like
crash a Miss Chinatown pageant

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.600
is funny in the context of

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.830
LA Asian-American culture.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:55.020
For most people, the beauty pageant queens

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.288
who are crashed on don't
find it very funny,

00:05:57.288 --> 00:05:59.130
and the people running the
pageant don't think it's funny.

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:02.930
But (laughs) they get that archetype

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:04.100
and that I'm stereotyping it.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:05.270
Now if I show up in some town

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:06.440
where there's no Miss Chinatown pageant,

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.270
dressed as a fake Miss Chinatown,

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:11.230
and I have tried this,
people get really confused,

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.080
and they're like, is this
really a beauty queen.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:14.933
What's happening?

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.560
And that's what the issue was
with this show that I pitched

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:25.660
was I was used to making
these guerrilla projects

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:29.140
where I have a very specific
audience that doesn't

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:33.410
necessarily know the same rubric that I'm

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.780
dealing with nationally.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:40.180
And truTV very much is a
flyover state comedy network,

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:42.530
and they're kind of sheepish about naming

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:45.300
certain political things, and it felt like

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.210
oh, all these things I
can do in the world of LA

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:53.930
are this big for my very patient
college educated audience.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:58.030
But people still need to react to this and

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.830
it's almost they can't
conceive of what is she doing.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:06.370
We need an explanation of
why she is like this first

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:09.690
before we can even watch her do pranks

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.490
because they're reading me
through a very specific lens.

00:07:14.187 --> 00:07:16.300
So that...

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.150
So I'm drawn to guerrilla work
because it doesn't require

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.160
me getting permission to get into a space.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.520
I love the theater, the way
people perform unintentionally.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.090
I think politicians are
constantly performing.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:30.990
I think everyone who has
a social media account

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:31.933
is performing.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:36.370
And so I love subverting
those performances.

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:37.640
I think we've all...

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.850
My whole theory is we are all
realty TV stars at this point.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:42.040
Whether we want to or not,

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:44.513
we all have the ability
to cancel or be canceled.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:47.790
And it's a scary, frightening thing.

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:49.460
We can scream back and feel...

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:52.840
We can tweet back at Trump
and feel like I did something

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:56.550
when (laughs) maybe I didn't,

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:57.910
did or didn't.

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:01.520
But yeah, that's my interest in that work.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:04.410
But I was really drawn to artists who,

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:05.600
as I dug through the names,

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:06.433
I was like, they're all white.

00:08:06.433 --> 00:08:09.733
Michael Moore, Nathan
Fielder, Sacha Baron Cohen,

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:13.070
The Yes Men who do guerrilla work

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:15.080
and do amazing political work,

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:17.240
and I'm like, where are the
examples of people of color

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:19.870
who are able to invade
spaces in the same way

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:23.170
and at least get commercial recognition or

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:25.283
those rewards.

00:08:25.283 --> 00:08:26.363
And I wasn't seeing it.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.400
- [Albert] So we want to also
get the students on camera

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.048
to have you answer questions directly.

00:08:35.048 --> 00:08:36.950
So we're gonna start with Amy.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:38.160
Amy, you gonna jump on,

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:39.160
turn your camera on?

00:08:40.107 --> 00:08:41.130
- [Kristina] Hi, Amy.
- [Amy] Hi.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:42.860
Hi. Thank you for being here.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:46.410
So my question is related
to the topic of audience.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:49.470
So do you write and
perform for a particular

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:53.130
audience in mind, and if
so, do you consider how

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:54.790
intentions may be perceived differently

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:57.833
by other audiences who do
watch your work in good faith?

00:08:59.773 --> 00:09:02.560
- [Kristina] (sighs) I think
about myself as an audience

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:07.290
member, and I would try
to write something that I

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:09.960
would feel proud of.

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:14.220
There's a certain brand of
humor that I feel panders,

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.579
and I don't want to write that.

00:09:16.579 --> 00:09:19.450
I want to surprise myself
in the writing process

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.142
and feel like, yeah,
I'd sit and watch that,

00:09:22.142 --> 00:09:23.820
and I'm not just delivering up

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:27.013
accent jokes or whatever.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:29.870
I do think about the fact
that I will be touring

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:33.563
colleges a lot because that's
in the venues I normally tour.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:37.310
That's why I don't have a 6,000 pound set

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:38.890
that needs to be freighted in.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:40.870
That's why I sew my own set pieces

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:42.940
that can roll up and show...

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:44.632
If you give me the crappiest caf...

00:09:44.632 --> 00:09:45.490
And this is what happened.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:47.590
My other shows would have tech.

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:50.510
I show up to a venue,
and it's a cafeteria,

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:52.067
and I'm like, "Oh, boy."

00:09:52.067 --> 00:09:53.930
(laughs) And so for this particular show,

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:57.800
I think I was like, "Well, I
don't want to use any video."

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.290
Then of course the great irony
is I couldn't even tour it

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:01.950
because of the pandemic,

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:05.303
and video ended up
being the actual medium.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.800
Yeah, I do think about
what I have access to,

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:12.290
and the situations I'm
probably gonna be thrown into,

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:15.760
and how to work with those parameters.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:18.053
This is one of the first videos,

00:10:18.053 --> 00:10:20.530
of pieces in a while that has no video

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:21.470
because I've always had some sort of

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:22.727
projection or something, and I was like,

00:10:22.727 --> 00:10:24.680
"I'm so sick of relying on

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:29.230
a video projector which
might go kaput on me."

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:31.280
But we did add some stuff
for this because we can.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:32.980
We added the pictures of the dog,

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:34.043
and the suffragettes.

00:10:35.232 --> 00:10:37.932
That was something that was
not part of the live show.

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:43.010
Does that answer?

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:43.960
Any more questions?

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:48.620
- [Quan] Along that line,
Isabelle has another question

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:51.380
about audience and reception to your work.

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:52.363
Isabelle, go ahead.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:57.700
- [Isabelle] Hi, thank
you so much for being here

00:10:57.700 --> 00:10:58.890
and performing for us.

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:00.534
- [Kristina] Yeah, thank you.

00:11:00.534 --> 00:11:01.367
- [Isabelle] It's really transformed

00:11:01.367 --> 00:11:03.631
what I thought was possible on Zoom.

00:11:03.631 --> 00:11:06.170
- [Kristina] Oh, yay. (laughs)

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:08.110
- [Isabelle] So my question for you was

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:10.710
there is often the critique
of Asian-American artists

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:13.340
and comedians perpetuating anti-Blackness

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.540
through their use of African
American Vernacular English

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:19.750
and appropriation of Black
culture and so forth.

00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:21.800
So how would you respond
to this criticism,

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:23.710
and how does it influence
your own approach

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:24.790
to comedy and performing?

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:27.450
- Yeah, that would've been a
really good question to ask me

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:29.520
for my last show, The Wong Street Journal.

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:31.090
I mean, you tell me if you...

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:33.720
I did track, and I
think about these things

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.040
because I read the critique articles,

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:35.873
and I'm like, "I don't
want to be the subject

00:11:35.873 --> 00:11:37.518
of one of those articles."

00:11:37.518 --> 00:11:39.833
(laughs)

00:11:39.833 --> 00:11:41.530
But I think it's definitely something

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:43.270
that I've been thinking about a lot more

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:45.110
in my work in the last few years

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:50.100
because of the public conversation around.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:51.447
My last show was The Wong Street Journal,

00:11:51.447 --> 00:11:54.210
and I actually went to
Uganda and made a rap album.

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:55.750
You want to talk about every land mine

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:58.630
you can possibly fall
into for that criticism,

00:11:58.630 --> 00:11:59.783
I was in it.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:03.410
And I didn't intentionally
make a rap album.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:04.340
I literally meant...

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:05.760
I was going to volunteer,

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.160
and I thought I was gonna
write a play about micro-loans,

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.980
and ended up meeting these guys.

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:13.850
And it was interesting
because hip-hip and rap

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:17.460
is very much an American genre

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.840
that has taken over everywhere.

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:22.870
I had a lot of critics who
didn't watch the full show,

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:24.770
but I did feel like the,

00:12:24.770 --> 00:12:27.590
in thinking about
anti-Blackness in the show...

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:32.020
Someone had once called out
that using the term hella

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:34.420
is using African American Vernacular,

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:37.590
but I learned that from
Asian fobs in middle school

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:38.450
in the school yard.

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:39.585
They would say hella.

00:12:39.585 --> 00:12:41.330
I was like, "What? I
can't use that anymore?"

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:42.783
It's so varied to me.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:48.000
So I guess I think for me I don't know

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:51.300
that I can remove
everything from my language,

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.670
but I do want to at least set up in

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:58.980
the very critical first
scene or 10, 15 minutes

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.420
as you get to know me that
I have a certain awareness

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:05.960
around certain issues of race
that I am actively trying,

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.920
even if I don't get everything right,

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:11.453
I am actively thinking about that.

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:15.740
I don't know if that
answers your question,

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:18.738
but I do think about it.

00:13:18.738 --> 00:13:22.220
I feel confused where some of this goes

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:23.470
because some of it is so...

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:25.010
Some of these things, which is considered

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:26.920
African American
Vernacular are so embedded

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:28.100
in how I already communicate

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:32.852
I think just from having lived
in the city and it didn't...

00:13:32.852 --> 00:13:33.685
I never...

00:13:33.685 --> 00:13:34.893
I don't know.

00:13:34.893 --> 00:13:35.726
I don't know where it came from,

00:13:35.726 --> 00:13:38.136
and then someone explains
that this is actually

00:13:38.136 --> 00:13:39.124
an AAV term.

00:13:39.124 --> 00:13:40.541
I'm like, "What?"

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:44.560
Yeah, I don't have a
complete answer to that,

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.870
but I do think that it is something that

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:50.010
comedians need to get
smarter about addressing

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:52.940
when they're called on it.

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:59.620
Yeah, that's the shift that
I hope happens is that...

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:01.720
I hope it doesn't mean

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:03.660
suddenly erasing everything in their set,

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:06.733
but at least recognizing
that this is coming up.

00:14:08.195 --> 00:14:10.660
(sighs) I feel like that
was a disappointing answer,

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:11.742
but that's what I have.

00:14:11.742 --> 00:14:12.575
Chayton?

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:15.570
- [Chayton] Yeah, question from the

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:17.180
acting/performance side of it.

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:19.940
What, to shift gears a little bit,

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:22.800
what differences have you found for...

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:24.850
I mean, I've been dealing
with this in my own work.

00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:27.610
But what differences have
you seen for a Zoom audience

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:29.431
and digital for camera work versus-

00:14:29.431 --> 00:14:31.130
- [Kristina] So many.
- [Chayton] something live,

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:33.990
actually being able to
hear the audience laugh?

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:36.330
- [Kristina] It's hard, it's hard.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:39.020
Luckily, this premiered for an audience

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:42.239
in what was the greatest
show of my life in February.

00:14:42.239 --> 00:14:44.389
I felt like I was in
my own comedy special.

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:48.167
So when shutdown happened, Diana's like,

00:14:48.167 --> 00:14:49.450
"We could probably still do it online."

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:50.743
I'm like, "No."

00:14:50.743 --> 00:14:52.320
(laughs) How are we gonna go from

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:54.563
all this screaming to silence?

00:14:55.555 --> 00:14:57.800
And the pandemic wiped all
the lines out of my head,

00:14:57.800 --> 00:14:59.770
so I had to re-watch the video

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:02.750
of me performing, and that helped me hear

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:05.770
the pauses and gaps for
laughter and anticipate it.

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:08.853
I'm not holding as long,
and they were long pauses.

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:11.553
So that helped.

00:15:12.429 --> 00:15:14.088
But I think it's

00:15:14.088 --> 00:15:17.360
thinking about the shift
that Diana helped direct me,

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:19.370
that it's not like I'm not projecting

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:21.120
like Kimberly Guilfoyle at the RNC,

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:23.920
instead of (laughs)...

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:26.600
It's like, hey, it's just you at home

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:27.610
and me at home.

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:29.500
It's much more intimate.

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:30.950
So that's the...

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:33.200
That has been a big blocking shift,

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:36.730
and the fact that rather
than clean up my house,

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:38.150
and pretend it's a neutral space,

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:40.800
is to use some of the natural
dramaturgy of the house

00:15:41.803 --> 00:15:45.700
because we can't have a light
change or shift or whatever.

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:47.560
And I still have to live in this space,

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:50.410
so we just use it to create new scenes and

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:53.320
colors and shifts, and then...

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:54.770
And this is what my proudest thing is,

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:56.240
is I'm like...

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:57.270
I don't have to just remember lines.

00:15:57.270 --> 00:15:59.030
I have to remember to move things.

00:15:59.030 --> 00:16:00.330
So this is my system.

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:02.150
It's a chair with two Tupperwares

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:04.763
that I put this on.

00:16:04.763 --> 00:16:07.020
And so it's not just
remember a line and walk.

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:08.280
I have to remember to move this

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:11.450
and (laughs) do stuff like that.

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:13.640
It's a different vocabulary.

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:15.660
I'm really glad we got
to premier it before.

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:17.650
I don't know what it's
like to premier a piece.

00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:19.330
I mean, I do because I
work I'm working on this

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:20.800
sweatshop overlord piece, but

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:25.008
yeah, I think it's...

00:16:25.008 --> 00:16:26.253
It's crazy.

00:16:27.477 --> 00:16:29.500
I had to learn three ways to do this.

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:31.010
One for a live audience, one for my house,

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:33.960
and then we shot this on Monday
for cameras in a theater.

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:34.800
But that...

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:37.590
I also had to focus.

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:41.800
It was a hybrid between the
live show and the Zoom show.

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:44.560
Rather than look at empty
seats where no one's sitting,

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:47.093
I would just directly
address a big camera.

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:53.200
- So I think Gabriella's
next with a question.

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:55.690
- [Gabriella] Yes.

00:16:55.690 --> 00:16:58.390
First off wanted to thank
you so much for this.

00:16:58.390 --> 00:16:59.223
- [Kristina] Thank you.

00:16:59.223 --> 00:17:02.320
- [Gabriella] I had a question
specifically concerning

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.150
your TV pilot and the
distillation of comedy

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:06.693
to whiter audiences.

00:17:07.929 --> 00:17:11.990
So you noted how you had
to tone down your comedy

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:14.750
and have your culture
become the butt of the joke

00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:17.013
in order to appeal to white audiences.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:22.370
So along those lines, I was
wondering how you think media

00:17:22.370 --> 00:17:24.910
and racial representation work together.

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:27.070
Do you think that racial
representation in media,

00:17:27.070 --> 00:17:29.970
especially in terms of
comedy, is worth pursuing

00:17:29.970 --> 00:17:31.820
when you have to change
it so much just to make it

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:33.323
make sense to white people?

00:17:34.224 --> 00:17:38.360
- [Kristina] (groans) I think
it's a pipeline issue of power

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:39.693
and who's in charge.

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:42.343
It was clear.

00:17:42.343 --> 00:17:44.310
I think I could've had a successful pilot

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:47.220
if I got to hire people who...

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:51.273
I mean, it was just hard.

00:17:51.273 --> 00:17:53.320
It was all white gatekeepers
in the situation.

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:54.350
They were so nervous.

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:55.440
It was like, you know...

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:58.350
I think you know who I am, but (laughs)

00:17:58.350 --> 00:18:00.450
but you're so nervous around...

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:02.610
It's like they didn't even like the idea

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:04.020
of me being too political,

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:05.760
and said it was a do-gooder.

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:08.873
They even wiped that down in the pilot.

00:18:10.097 --> 00:18:12.750
And it was just like I think
things have to say stuff.

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:13.790
You can't...

00:18:14.979 --> 00:18:16.950
(sighs) I think trying to pussyfoot around

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:18.940
words like feminism and stuff like that,

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:22.640
and just trying to pretend
that for middle America

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:25.670
it looks like some sort
of morpheous vague thing

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:26.940
won't work.

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:29.660
And that's what they were trying to do,

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:31.200
rather than name exact issues.

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:33.670
That it was just like,
Kristina just likes to fix

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:35.230
and help people.

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:38.320
And that became kind of dangerous,

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:40.850
in the sense that I felt like...

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:43.170
I became confused.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:44.710
Wait, do I even care about this issue,

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:47.940
or am I Dennis the Menace
that just comes and

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:51.580
screws up what real activists
are trying to do here?

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:53.030
It was just a really confusing process.

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:54.730
I think we just...

00:18:54.730 --> 00:18:58.070
We need more people of color
making decisions at the top

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:02.253
and making clear where tastes lie.

00:19:03.158 --> 00:19:06.040
I feel like as frustrated as I am

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:08.960
that we have to go to war
with everything on Twitter,

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:10.083
it seems to be one of the few things

00:19:10.083 --> 00:19:12.530
that can actually move the needle.

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:14.720
Like the Oscars so white hashtag

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:17.360
actually helped shift to get more people

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:21.140
of color in the Oscars in the voting pool

00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:22.203
and stuff like that.

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:25.600
But it was a tough thing
when I was in that room

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:27.560
because it was like I can't take the angry

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:30.930
college activist approach
and go to war with everyone

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:33.770
in this room because I'm still
trying to work with them,

00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:36.640
and they still at the end
of the day have this power.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:38.420
That's what is so hard and frustrating

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:40.870
is I would just find myself just smiling

00:19:40.870 --> 00:19:42.423
and going along with stuff,

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:48.630
and I had very few allies.

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:50.770
The only allies were the white women.

00:19:50.770 --> 00:19:52.399
That was the closest thing I could find

00:19:52.399 --> 00:19:53.530
to vet for me.

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:54.880
And they missed things too.

00:19:56.890 --> 00:20:01.070
So yeah, I wish there were
more execs at the top who...

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:05.500
And a lot of it is those
white execs can hand hold...

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:06.380
Not the white execs.

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:09.700
The execs of color can help hand hold

00:20:09.700 --> 00:20:11.910
the people who are like,
ooh, I don't know this,

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:13.100
and like, no, no, no, it's gonna be fine,

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:13.933
it's gonna be fine.

00:20:13.933 --> 00:20:15.520
And that's how stuff gets moved in.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:18.500
After radical cram school,
the same web series that

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:21.550
Alex Jones went to war with me about,

00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:23.220
Disney called us.

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:25.110
We were all like, "Oh my
gosh, are we gonna be able to

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:28.860
buy big houses in Malibu,"
and it was the low,

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:31.250
the low rung folks who work with the VPs,

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:32.830
and they were like, "We're all about

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:34.600
dismantling the patriarchy in here.

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:36.410
This is too much, but
what else do you have?"

00:20:36.410 --> 00:20:37.630
And I loved that.

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:39.280
It literally felt like
they opened the curtain,

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:41.405
and let the rats run through.

00:20:41.405 --> 00:20:44.322
(laughs) And they were women of color,

00:20:44.322 --> 00:20:46.843
but their higher ups were white folks.

00:20:49.325 --> 00:20:50.650
So it's a slow, long process,

00:20:50.650 --> 00:20:51.620
but it needs a...

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:53.870
The whole pipeline needs to shift on up,

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:55.360
so it's not even just the talent,

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:57.243
but those who make decisions.

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:00.663
- [Quan] Great.

00:21:00.663 --> 00:21:03.273
Next off we have Dora
following up with a question.

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:06.490
- [Dora] Hi, thank you so much.

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:10.343
On the note of Alex Jones,

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:15.660
what is your best advice
as a woman in comedy

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:17.240
and a woman of color in comedy

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:20.650
on how not to give in to people like that,

00:21:20.650 --> 00:21:23.017
how to identify whose
criticisms (inaudible).

00:21:25.583 --> 00:21:27.610
- I mean, you think about who's
the audience you care about

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:28.910
and play for those crowds.

00:21:32.388 --> 00:21:34.240
When I get invited to play
for more white spaces,

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:37.120
I get nervous, and I don't
turn down necessarily,

00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:38.820
but I try to get as much
information as possible

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:41.000
to figure out what I'm going to present

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:43.820
so that it goes over well.

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:45.210
But I also tend to play theater spaces

00:21:45.210 --> 00:21:48.629
because those audiences don't heckle you

00:21:48.629 --> 00:21:51.513
(laughs) whereas some of
these other spaces will.

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:53.670
But not get...

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:56.660
I mean, I've been trolled for 20 years

00:21:56.660 --> 00:21:58.853
since my bigbadchinesemama.com.

00:22:00.836 --> 00:22:02.750
And I've had people send
me hate mail on and off,

00:22:02.750 --> 00:22:03.793
and it took that.

00:22:04.782 --> 00:22:06.930
That was the crowning moment of trolldom

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:11.210
for me to go, you know what,

00:22:11.210 --> 00:22:12.600
I made it through 20 years of this.

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:13.850
I can make it another 20.

00:22:16.090 --> 00:22:17.870
And if you're gonna make an enemy,

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:19.320
that's the enemy you want to make.

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:21.560
And we also, among our producers,

00:22:21.560 --> 00:22:23.510
we were like, "What are we gonna do?

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:24.343
Do we respond to this?

00:22:24.343 --> 00:22:25.176
Do we just delete stuff?

00:22:25.176 --> 00:22:26.009
Do we hide?"

00:22:26.009 --> 00:22:28.120
And then we were like, "Let's
shoot a second season."

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:29.680
So it's just like...

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:31.890
But it's taken this.

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:33.406
I don't think 20 years
I would've had this...

00:22:33.406 --> 00:22:35.660
I guess would've panicked
and maybe just disappeared.

00:22:35.660 --> 00:22:38.130
But I think they want you to go away,

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:39.780
and you have to be like...

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:41.360
It's a lot of self talk.

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:42.600
It's been a lot of self help books.

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:45.250
It's like I still have
to say yes to myself

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:46.360
before I leave the door every day,

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:47.880
before I want anyone to say yes,

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:50.170
before I expect anyone to say yes to me

00:22:50.170 --> 00:22:51.470
or give me an opportunity.

00:22:51.470 --> 00:22:52.303
And I have to...

00:22:52.303 --> 00:22:53.820
You have to be somewhat
delusional in the sense like,

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:55.930
no, I'm just gonna do this,

00:22:55.930 --> 00:22:57.730
and there's gonna be lots of haters.

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:01.780
It's a terrible use of energy

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:03.630
to try to bring down people's careers

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:04.463
and cancel people.

00:23:04.463 --> 00:23:05.950
I actually try to avoid canceling people

00:23:05.950 --> 00:23:07.400
as much as possible,

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:09.550
if I can't find a way
to be funny about it.

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:12.043
Yeah.

00:23:12.043 --> 00:23:14.140
But I just go, "All right.

00:23:14.140 --> 00:23:16.447
That's a great use of
your time, you dum-dum."

00:23:18.529 --> 00:23:19.950
Yeah, but it's...

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:22.700
I understand that it's not an easy thing,

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:26.730
but (laughs) I guess 20 years
of building this thick skin,

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.020
I'm just like...

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:30.070
No one's showing up at the
house, so I'm like, "Okay, well

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:31.710
I guess that's okay."

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:33.393
Gonna lock down my info.

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:37.737
It also goes in waves.

00:23:37.737 --> 00:23:40.830
They get mad for two weeks or a few days,

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:42.300
and then they move on to the next person

00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:43.550
to get mad at and attack.

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:47.900
I know, it's so terrible.

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:49.890
It's just sort of like,
eh, it's what it is.

00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:52.870
But I guess it just...

00:23:52.870 --> 00:23:54.430
Yeah.

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:55.630
It just makes more work.

00:23:57.370 --> 00:23:58.840
I'll make more shows from this that people

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:00.390
will probably be pissed off at.

00:24:01.381 --> 00:24:03.240
(laughs) I'm just like, "Okay."

00:24:05.540 --> 00:24:07.640
- [Albert] Our next question is from Khue.

00:24:08.547 --> 00:24:11.260
- [Khue] Hi, I loved
your performance today.

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:12.620
- [Kristina] Thank you, Khue.

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:16.820
- [Khue] I was wondering if
you know the reactions of

00:24:16.820 --> 00:24:19.810
how older Asian American
people engage with your comedy

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:22.530
and what reactions are elicited from them.

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:25.810
And if you know the one
Facebook support group

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:28.940
that's Asian Americans
With Republican Parents

00:24:28.940 --> 00:24:29.773
Support Group-

00:24:29.773 --> 00:24:31.310
- [Kristina] Oh, I
don't have one of those.

00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:32.143
I don't have republican parents,

00:24:32.143 --> 00:24:33.170
so I haven't had to join that.

00:24:33.170 --> 00:24:35.078
But I love that there's a...

00:24:35.078 --> 00:24:37.670
- [Khue] (laughs) How do you think...

00:24:37.670 --> 00:24:39.010
Can comedy...

00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:40.570
How can comedy be used to address

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:42.350
political tensions between
different generations,

00:24:42.350 --> 00:24:44.020
especially if there are language barriers

00:24:44.020 --> 00:24:45.880
or different...

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:47.520
- [Kristina] I wonder if my
mom is still in the room.

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:48.353
Let me see.

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:50.860
Hey Mommy, you want to get on?

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:51.693
No?

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:54.120
- [Gwen] Nope. (laughs)

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:55.100
- [Kristina] Okay, she
don't want to get on.

00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:57.173
Okay. (laughs)

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:02.110
But I feel like these shows are
my most honest conversations

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:05.383
with my parents.

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:07.223
I don't know.

00:25:07.223 --> 00:25:09.270
I know that's a crazy
answer because not everyone

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:11.633
invites their folks to
come see their work.

00:25:14.246 --> 00:25:16.463
(laughs) It's funny how she got on.

00:25:17.918 --> 00:25:19.700
That was my mother's cameo, everybody.

00:25:19.700 --> 00:25:20.533
I don't know.

00:25:20.533 --> 00:25:21.366
I think

00:25:23.090 --> 00:25:24.230
how does it just bridge.

00:25:24.230 --> 00:25:25.630
I guess you...

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:28.940
If anything, it's first to...

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:31.460
I love that the support
group is other people

00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:34.700
with republican parents
trying to comfort each other.

00:25:34.700 --> 00:25:36.580
So maybe you make work first
to just comfort each other.

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:39.380
Like, "Oh geez, why do we live with this?"

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:41.650
You're not gonna convert
them necessarily over night,

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:43.720
or get them to switch sides.

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:47.060
But I think there is something wonderful

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:49.690
in at least going, "Okay, okay.

00:25:49.690 --> 00:25:51.440
We have each other." (laughs)

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:52.520
We might not have our parents right now,

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:53.670
but we have each other.

00:25:56.778 --> 00:25:59.920
I don't know, but it's a community

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:03.223
we're slowly building of comfort.

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:07.820
- Thanks, Kristina.

00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:09.530
So happy to see your mom here.

00:26:09.530 --> 00:26:12.480
Welcome, and (laughs) it's
great to always have...

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.760
Seriously, I think I
wish sometimes that all,

00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:17.620
for all of us in the things that we do,

00:26:17.620 --> 00:26:21.610
we can show our parents more frequently.

00:26:21.610 --> 00:26:25.530
On that note I think maybe
next up would be Hector

00:26:25.530 --> 00:26:29.030
who has a question about...

00:26:29.030 --> 00:26:31.823
Well, Hector can ask the question.

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:36.340
- [Hector] Hi, thank you
for your performance.

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:39.530
My question's more of your
influences growing up,

00:26:39.530 --> 00:26:42.660
both in terms of maybe
professional comedians,

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:44.820
or influences in your family.

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:46.060
- [Kristina] Yeah.

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:49.440
My mother's actually quite
hilarious, and my father is...

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:51.410
He sold insurance,

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:53.460
but they would have these parties,

00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:55.900
retirement parties, and they
would always do these skits.

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:57.250
And even when he turned 40 years old,

00:26:57.250 --> 00:27:01.220
he lip synced a Johnny
Mathis song to my mother.

00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:04.143
And I just think they're
actually quite funny.

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:07.170
Aren't you funny, Mommy?

00:27:08.330 --> 00:27:09.992
Okay, anyway.

00:27:09.992 --> 00:27:10.825
- [Hector] How has...

00:27:10.825 --> 00:27:11.658
Sorry, how has your mom-

00:27:11.658 --> 00:27:13.227
- [Kristina] I think she is. (laughs)

00:27:13.227 --> 00:27:16.070
Just really dry things that...

00:27:16.070 --> 00:27:19.143
I think it was advice, but I
think it was just so hilarious.

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:24.530
None of this advice actually
works if I'm not a dead person

00:27:24.530 --> 00:27:25.530
hiding all the time.

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:31.143
I wish you'd share a
zinger with them, Mommy.

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:34.520
- [Gwen] No, that's okay. (laughs)

00:27:34.520 --> 00:27:35.353
- [Kristina] Okay.

00:27:35.353 --> 00:27:37.880
All right, okay, so (laughs)

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:41.210
what else?

00:27:41.210 --> 00:27:42.540
Oh, Lucille Ball.

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:46.003
I loved Perfect Strangers, Mork and Mindy,

00:27:47.570 --> 00:27:50.610
Three's Company, all
those really cheesy shows.

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:51.620
I grew up...

00:27:51.620 --> 00:27:53.053
TGIF, Full House.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:57.110
And then when I got to
college, I started to unleash

00:27:57.110 --> 00:28:01.410
my angry side and was looking
at this whole side of myself

00:28:01.410 --> 00:28:03.180
I was repressing this whole time,

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:06.710
and was so into this dark
slow moving performance art

00:28:06.710 --> 00:28:10.140
where people perform in
bathrooms and draw on themselves.

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:12.670
There's no beginning
and no end to the show.

00:28:12.670 --> 00:28:14.683
I was like, "Yeah, I want to do that."

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:19.440
And I was just really
fascinated with this genre

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:22.243
of work they call endurance
work or performance art.

00:28:24.050 --> 00:28:26.080
I think somewhere between comedy and that

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:29.980
is where I ended up making...

00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:31.550
I still do the show that I can repeat,

00:28:31.550 --> 00:28:33.260
whereas a lot of these
performance art shows

00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:34.180
you can only perform once

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:36.040
because you only get yourself
shot in the arm once.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:39.920
You can only (laughs) shave your head

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:41.373
while you read a poem once.

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:44.800
Yeah, so I...

00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:48.132
Yeah, I feel like it's a lot
of that, but I'm really...

00:28:48.132 --> 00:28:49.560
I love...

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:52.030
The show I pitched was like
I want to do Michael Moore

00:28:52.030 --> 00:28:53.770
meets Nathan for you.

00:28:53.770 --> 00:28:56.553
And I want to go out and prank people,

00:29:00.261 --> 00:29:01.390
and have that edited together

00:29:01.390 --> 00:29:05.593
and have this amazing final
TV show moment that folks see.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:08.370
- [Hector] Thank you.

00:29:09.287 --> 00:29:11.437
- [Albert] So our next
question's from Sam.

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:15.931
- [Sam] Hello.

00:29:15.931 --> 00:29:17.420
It's another question of just about

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:18.910
how you envision your audience.

00:29:18.910 --> 00:29:20.990
I know this show is definitely
very focused on the US,

00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:23.130
but a lot of your other performance work

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:24.870
has been around Asian Americans,

00:29:24.870 --> 00:29:26.520
and I was wondering if you've had any

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:29.850
responses from Asians in
Asia or other diaspora places

00:29:29.850 --> 00:29:31.890
and how they've responded to your work.

00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:33.000
- [Kristina] Yeah, I'll be quite honest.

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:34.320
My show does not...

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:37.270
Or my past work, and I think part of me

00:29:37.270 --> 00:29:39.110
had this dream of I want
to tour internationally,

00:29:39.110 --> 00:29:41.660
and I was like, "Wow, the
Asian American identity

00:29:41.660 --> 00:29:45.891
does not really translate
well in other..."

00:29:45.891 --> 00:29:49.440
You've got to take it
way down and real basic.

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:51.370
The example I name is
Edinburgh Fringe Festival,

00:29:51.370 --> 00:29:54.199
which is this big fringe
festival in Scotland,

00:29:54.199 --> 00:29:57.290
and, I described them as 90s
solo shows about identity,

00:29:57.290 --> 00:29:58.772
were doing really well there.

00:29:58.772 --> 00:30:00.940
This is a fictitious name of a show,

00:30:00.940 --> 00:30:03.970
but like the I'm Pakistani.

00:30:03.970 --> 00:30:05.963
I'm Pakistani in a woman's show.

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:08.760
It wasn't very good.

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:10.350
These kind of shows are like,

00:30:10.350 --> 00:30:11.510
these are the different kinds of...

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:14.901
Did you know we have different
kinds of personalities?

00:30:14.901 --> 00:30:16.340
(laughs) These multi character shows

00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:19.770
that I thought is not
the aesthetic that I like

00:30:19.770 --> 00:30:20.990
went over so well there.

00:30:20.990 --> 00:30:23.120
And I'm just like, this is so stupid.

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:26.753
It's like people going, "Oh,
race," for the first time.

00:30:28.010 --> 00:30:30.140
So I did a show called Going
Green The Wong Way over there,

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:32.407
and the audience was like, "Who cares?"

00:30:34.530 --> 00:30:37.020
I did bring The Wong
Street Journal to Nigeria.

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:37.853
That was...

00:30:38.940 --> 00:30:40.440
Some of the audience appreciated,

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:42.690
those who worked for NGOs and stuff,

00:30:42.690 --> 00:30:45.580
folks who were living in the
in between appreciated it.

00:30:45.580 --> 00:30:49.010
Nigerians have a very
different way of watching

00:30:49.010 --> 00:30:50.650
US theater.

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:52.530
They watch it like it's a music concert,

00:30:52.530 --> 00:30:53.810
so they'll walk in and out.

00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:55.230
At least my show.

00:30:55.230 --> 00:30:58.460
They'll just text openly,
take selfies with each other,

00:30:58.460 --> 00:31:01.230
and I'm still talking,
and I'm like, "Oh my gosh,

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:03.007
this is so stressful."

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:06.800
And a lot of the...

00:31:06.800 --> 00:31:10.350
Just a lot of the context
just didn't really...

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:12.150
They were very nice to me,

00:31:12.150 --> 00:31:14.073
but it just didn't translate.

00:31:15.151 --> 00:31:18.800
So I think for this show,
I was just like, I'm fine.

00:31:18.800 --> 00:31:21.070
I would rather play the
audiences that really

00:31:21.960 --> 00:31:24.700
I feel like I'm myself
in and an audience can

00:31:26.170 --> 00:31:27.130
dig in on.

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:28.760
And that's...

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:31.083
I don't need to tour
this all over the world.

00:31:33.070 --> 00:31:36.640
I'm fine to just play this
for the context of Americans

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:39.040
who understand that we're in
an election cycle right now,

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:40.210
and that's what this was for.

00:31:40.210 --> 00:31:42.340
For me was a show that would...

00:31:42.340 --> 00:31:45.930
And it's a dream came true,
except it's a global pandemic

00:31:45.930 --> 00:31:47.853
and maybe fascism coming next week.

00:31:50.132 --> 00:31:53.230
But I wanted it to really
play in this context,

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:55.740
and for an American audience,

00:31:55.740 --> 00:31:58.240
for people dealing with
voting for the first time,

00:31:58.240 --> 00:31:59.640
which is a lot of you folks.

00:32:01.390 --> 00:32:02.993
That's what I wanted this to be.

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:08.060
- [Quan] Okay Kristina,
last question for the night.

00:32:08.060 --> 00:32:11.450
I'm sure our audience are
curious about what's next for you

00:32:14.570 --> 00:32:17.403
in terms of performances
and work that you're doing.

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:21.820
- [Kristina] Oh, what am I doing next?

00:32:21.820 --> 00:32:22.653
Sorry.

00:32:23.703 --> 00:32:24.900
- [Albert] What other
projects you might be

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:26.310
working on right now, what do you got-

00:32:26.310 --> 00:32:27.966
- [Kristina] Oh, yeah.

00:32:27.966 --> 00:32:29.460
It's running the Auntie Sewing Squad,

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:33.710
which is a very different
kind of performance

00:32:33.710 --> 00:32:36.080
in that I sewed all this stuff,

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:37.950
and then I, first week of the pandemic,

00:32:37.950 --> 00:32:41.100
was like, "I'm gonna sew masks
because I'm not essential.

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:42.940
But if I sew masks, I'll be essential."

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:47.420
And seven months later, our
group is still sewing masks

00:32:47.420 --> 00:32:50.700
which is ridiculous
because we live in America.

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:53.470
Why are we sewing masks for people?

00:32:53.470 --> 00:32:56.380
But unfortunately, it's
like systemic racism.

00:32:56.380 --> 00:32:59.860
To me it was like this
gesture of all health matters.

00:32:59.860 --> 00:33:02.980
But then it becomes very clear that yes,

00:33:02.980 --> 00:33:04.460
all our health matters,
but there are people

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:06.130
whose health is actually way worse shape

00:33:06.130 --> 00:33:07.100
because of this pandemic,

00:33:07.100 --> 00:33:08.610
and they tend to be
indigenous communities,

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:12.110
Black communities,
incarcerated communities,

00:33:12.110 --> 00:33:14.000
undocumented communities.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:16.910
And that's who we're still
working on supporting

00:33:16.910 --> 00:33:17.743
the health of.

00:33:17.743 --> 00:33:21.680
So we're doing this coat
drive, a warm coat drive

00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:22.513
for Standing Rock.

00:33:22.513 --> 00:33:23.590
I say this because you're in Connecticut

00:33:23.590 --> 00:33:25.280
and you might have gained so much

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:27.830
coronavirus pandemic weight

00:33:27.830 --> 00:33:29.320
that you have an extra winter coat.

00:33:29.320 --> 00:33:31.557
We have an address at
aunnitesewingsquad.com.

00:33:31.557 --> 00:33:34.580
You can directly mail
your old winter clothes

00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:36.390
to Standing Rock.

00:33:36.390 --> 00:33:38.070
We've just gotten really deep into this.

00:33:38.070 --> 00:33:40.090
I was just like, this was supposed to be

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:41.561
a two, three week stock gap,

00:33:41.561 --> 00:33:45.320
and suddenly hundreds of us are still

00:33:45.320 --> 00:33:48.090
running around and collecting things

00:33:48.090 --> 00:33:50.133
so that people don't freeze to death.

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:53.420
We're getting medical
supplies to Standing Rock.

00:33:53.420 --> 00:33:55.790
We're doing relief
drives in Navajo Nation.

00:33:55.790 --> 00:33:58.550
We sent a bunch of KN95s to farm workers

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:00.430
affected by the fires.

00:34:00.430 --> 00:34:02.420
That's a project, and I
think we're gonna be here

00:34:02.420 --> 00:34:03.460
till the end of the pandemic.

00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:06.203
We actually have a book
deal, which is crazy,

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:08.690
something I would have loved in my career

00:34:08.690 --> 00:34:09.523
outside of this,

00:34:09.523 --> 00:34:13.000
but it had to come down
to starting a shadow FEMA

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:13.933
to make happen.

00:34:14.947 --> 00:34:16.110
And then I'm working on a new show called

00:34:16.110 --> 00:34:17.630
Kristina Wong Sweatshop Overlord,

00:34:17.630 --> 00:34:20.230
which I hope will unfold
into a piece called

00:34:20.230 --> 00:34:22.690
the Auntie Retirement Party.

00:34:22.690 --> 00:34:23.860
So we've been talking about

00:34:23.860 --> 00:34:25.630
we joke a lot as aunties
that we're gonna one day

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:27.610
retire and meet each other,

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:28.750
but it's become really clear.

00:34:28.750 --> 00:34:30.910
It's like, yeah, we can
just retire and stop,

00:34:30.910 --> 00:34:32.890
but the problem will still be there.

00:34:32.890 --> 00:34:34.270
So what does it mean to actually retire

00:34:34.270 --> 00:34:35.184
and move away from it?

00:34:35.184 --> 00:34:36.850
I think this is a conversation
a lot of organizations

00:34:36.850 --> 00:34:39.950
are having, especially
theater organizations that

00:34:39.950 --> 00:34:42.890
they've been producing
shows at such a rapid pace

00:34:42.890 --> 00:34:45.380
that they haven't stopped
to check how white supremacy

00:34:45.380 --> 00:34:48.400
is embedded in their leadership structures

00:34:48.400 --> 00:34:49.500
and their funding structures

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:52.223
and their programming decision making.

00:34:54.281 --> 00:34:56.450
And now is this moment to
think what do we want to...

00:34:56.450 --> 00:34:58.220
How do we want to shift forward?

00:34:58.220 --> 00:34:59.830
And for me with the
aunties, I'm just like,

00:34:59.830 --> 00:35:02.150
what do we do with this community of care

00:35:02.150 --> 00:35:03.780
that we've created which
has been so powerful?

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:05.390
Do we continue?

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:08.870
Do we just walk away and that's it?

00:35:08.870 --> 00:35:10.890
Do we exist as a cultural forest?

00:35:10.890 --> 00:35:13.533
What does retirement
mean for a group like us?

00:35:14.950 --> 00:35:18.163
So that's a very conceptual
piece I'm stewing on right now.

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:22.740
- [Albert] Well Kristina,
thank you again so much

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:24.510
for this performance, for taking the time

00:35:24.510 --> 00:35:25.660
to answer our questions.

00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:27.720
Thanks to Diana and the rest of your crew

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:31.870
for making everything just
feel so seamless and tight.

00:35:31.870 --> 00:35:34.304
I can't believe you pulled it off.

00:35:34.304 --> 00:35:35.360
I think about a performance on Zoom,

00:35:35.360 --> 00:35:39.080
I kept asking my students
this must be tough, right?

00:35:39.080 --> 00:35:40.230
There's a thing about Yale students.

00:35:40.230 --> 00:35:42.010
They've all been in theater in some way

00:35:42.010 --> 00:35:42.843
when they were in high school.

00:35:42.843 --> 00:35:43.676
I have a thesis about that.

00:35:43.676 --> 00:35:45.430
- [Kristina] Yeah, yeah!

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:46.970
That's a very Yale thing.

00:35:46.970 --> 00:35:49.730
There's a lot of actors come out of Yale,

00:35:49.730 --> 00:35:51.320
and come into Yale.

00:35:51.320 --> 00:35:52.925
- [Albert] Yeah.
- [Kristina] Yeah.

00:35:52.925 --> 00:35:54.373
- We were talking about
that, the complexity

00:35:54.373 --> 00:35:55.230
of doing this in the Zoom.

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:56.413
It was fantastic.

00:35:57.710 --> 00:35:58.870
Yeah, thanks again.

00:35:58.870 --> 00:36:01.010
Quan, do you have any parting thoughts?

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:03.210
Thanks to the audience,
everyone who tuned it.

00:36:03.210 --> 00:36:04.170
Really appreciate you taking the time

00:36:04.170 --> 00:36:05.923
with the Zoom fatigue.

00:36:07.740 --> 00:36:09.290
- [Quan] No, I think Diana,

00:36:09.290 --> 00:36:11.660
you're gonna take us away, right?

00:36:11.660 --> 00:36:14.350
- [Diana] Yeah, yes, I can do that.

00:36:14.350 --> 00:36:17.180
Let me strike up some music.

00:36:17.180 --> 00:36:18.613
And I think as we head out,

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:20.820
just want to...

00:36:20.820 --> 00:36:23.330
The whole show is to go
encourage everybody to vote,

00:36:23.330 --> 00:36:24.163
so please vote, vote, vote.

00:36:24.163 --> 00:36:24.996
- [Kristina] Yes, vote.

00:36:24.996 --> 00:36:25.880
That's the biggest thing.

00:36:25.880 --> 00:36:28.130
- [Diana] Push everyone
who hasn't voted to vote.

00:36:28.130 --> 00:36:30.462
Doesn't matter what for, just
get your voice out there.

00:36:30.462 --> 00:36:31.360
- [Kristina] I have one more.

00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:32.380
If you missed...

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:34.180
If you want to recommend
the show to someone,

00:36:34.180 --> 00:36:36.670
this was the last show in
my house before elections,

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:39.530
but Center Theater Group is...

00:36:39.530 --> 00:36:41.470
We've filmed it on Monday,
and it's gonna be airing

00:36:41.470 --> 00:36:42.380
starting Friday.

00:36:42.380 --> 00:36:46.340
It's only 10 bucks, and you get
a 30 day window to watch it.

00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:48.968
Oh, oops, I sent it privately to Chayton.

00:36:48.968 --> 00:36:50.110
(Diana laughs)

00:36:50.110 --> 00:36:51.650
Sorry.

00:36:51.650 --> 00:36:54.880
- [Chayton] I mean, I was
gonna send it somewhere anyway.

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:57.053
- [Kristina] But please, yeah.

00:36:57.910 --> 00:36:59.550
This is a big experiment.

00:36:59.550 --> 00:37:00.987
Poor theater companies right now are like,

00:37:00.987 --> 00:37:02.450
"Are we TV networks?"

00:37:02.450 --> 00:37:04.870
And so I'm the first fully staged show

00:37:04.870 --> 00:37:06.530
they took a chance on.

00:37:06.530 --> 00:37:10.530
So please sing the praises because

00:37:10.530 --> 00:37:12.677
it's no small thing that they were like,

00:37:12.677 --> 00:37:16.040
"You, Kristina Wong, Asian woman

00:37:17.720 --> 00:37:19.510
whose opinions are what they are,

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:20.790
you're gonna be the first show

00:37:20.790 --> 00:37:22.920
that's fully staged in this series."

00:37:22.920 --> 00:37:25.393
I'd like to have a big gold opening,

00:37:26.285 --> 00:37:29.430
(laughs) and show their
support for this work.

00:37:29.430 --> 00:37:30.730
- [Diana] Yeah, and this way you can

00:37:30.730 --> 00:37:32.960
share it with your families
if you dug the show.

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:35.700
This is the easiest way to
share it with everyone else.

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:36.970
- [Kristina] Thank you, Jared.

00:37:36.970 --> 00:37:39.500
Thank you for your wonderful comments.

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:40.500
- [Diana] All right.

